This driver supports any braille display which conforms to the HID braille
display usage page (0X41). The USB Implementers Forum standardized this page in
HID Usage Tables Review Request 78 (HUTRR78). The driver has no knowledge of
particular models. It reads the HID report descriptor of the display at connect
time. The descriptor identifies the display and defines its report layout. A
new conforming display therefore needs no change to this driver.

The display must be reachable via HID. Name it explicitly:

   brltty -b hb -d hid:

Use brltty-hid to see what a display declares and how this driver reads it:

   brltty-hid --list-items --list-reports --show-braille-display

A report descriptor can also be inspected without the display. This helps with
bug reports:

   cp /sys/class/hidraw/hidraw0/device/report_descriptor display.hid
   brltty-hid --items-file display.hid --show-braille-display

The same tool shows what would be written to the cells. It also shows what an
input report would mean. Neither needs a display:

   brltty-hid --items-file display.hid --braille-cells '[125] 00 [12345678]'
   brltty-hid --items-file display.hid --braille-input '03 01 00'

On Linux, BRLTTY needs read/write access to the /dev/hidrawN device of the
display. See Autostart/Udev/hid.rules.

What is supported
================

Output
   The 8 dot braille cell (0X03) and 6 dot braille cell (0X04) usages of the
   first braille row (0X02) collection. Eight dots are preferred when a display
   declares both. Dots 7 and 8 are dropped when only six are available.

Input
   The braille keyboard dots (0X201 through 0X208), space (0X209), left space
   (0X20A), right space (0X20B), the joystick (0X210 through 0X214), the d-pad
   (0X215 through 0X219), panning (0X21A, 0X21B), and the rocker (0X21C through
   0X21E). Each of them can be bound by name. See Tables/Input/hb/.

Routing keys
   The router key (0X100) and row router key (0X101) usages of the router set 1
   (0X0FA), router set 2 (0X0FB), and router set 3 (0X0FC) collections. Only the
   first set found routes the cursor.

Number of braille cells (0X05)
   The number of cells of the row which the display uses. The driver honors it
   whenever the display sends it within an input report. Other screen readers
   do the same. The braille window is resized accordingly.

Screen reader identifier (0X07)
   Written to the display once, at connect time. HUTRR78 defines no values for
   it. The value comes from the screenReader= parameter. It defaults to 0.

Screen reader control (0X06)
   Offered as the ScreenReaderControl key group. A value can thus be bound to a
   command. HUTRR78 defines no values for it. None are bound by default.

Parameters
==========

cellCount
   Use this many cells rather than as many as the report descriptor declares.
   This is only needed for a display whose descriptor overstates its size.

screenReader
   The value to write to the screen reader identifier usage.

Known deviations from HUTRR78
=============================

These deviations follow what displays do. The implementations in NVDA and
BDIOHID established them. The sample report descriptor of HUTRR78 suggests
otherwise.

- The number of cells comes from the report count of the braille cell field.
  The number of braille cells usage (0X05) reports how many of them are in use.
  This may be fewer. The driver treats it as advisory.

- A display repeats the router key usage once per cell. It doesn't declare a
  usage range. The sample report descriptor in HUTRR78 doesn't work as written.
  Both forms are accepted.

Not supported
=============

- Bluetooth. Only HID over USB has been implemented.

- More than one braille row. A display which declares several rows is driven as
  though it had only the first one. The other rows are logged and ignored.

- Router keys which aren't declared consecutively within one report. No known
  display does this. Such keys are logged and ignored.
